  "content": {
    "id": "p141-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{x+2}$ and $g(x)=\\frac{x+2}{x+4}$. Find the domain of the following.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p141-q22-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(g(x))$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-\\infty,-4)\\cup\\left(-4,-\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\cup\\left(-\\frac{10}{3},\\infty\\right)$",
          "worked": "The inner function $g$ requires $x\\ne-4$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=f\\left(\\frac{x+2}{x+4}\\right)\\\\f(g(x))&=\\frac{\\frac{x+2}{x+4}-1}{\\frac{x+2}{x+4}+2}\\\\f(g(x))&=-\\frac{2}{3x+10}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe denominator also requires $3x+10\\ne0$, so $x\\ne-\\frac{10}{3}$. Both restrictions must hold.\n\n$$D=(-\\infty,-4)\\cup\\left(-4,-\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\cup\\left(-\\frac{10}{3},\\infty\\right).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-q22-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$g(f(x))$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(-\\infty,-2)\\cup\\left(-2,-\\frac{7}{5}\\right)\\cup\\left(-\\frac{7}{5},\\infty\\right)$",
          "worked": "The inner function $f$ requires $x\\ne-2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=g\\left(\\frac{x-1}{x+2}\\right)\\\\g(f(x))&=\\frac{\\frac{x-1}{x+2}+2}{\\frac{x-1}{x+2}+4}\\\\g(f(x))&=\\frac{3(x+1)}{5x+7}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe denominator also requires $5x+7\\ne0$, so $x\\ne-\\frac{7}{5}$. Both restrictions must hold.\n\n$$D=(-\\infty,-2)\\cup\\left(-2,-\\frac{7}{5}\\right)\\cup\\left(-\\frac{7}{5},\\infty\\right).$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
